text:
The diff removes the `^` anchor from `parseRegExp`. With the anchor, the whole string had to be a number followed by a unit; without it, `exec` will happily find a `<number><unit>` suffix anywhere in the string. So inputs like `'foo1kb'` or `'1kb2mb'`, which previously failed the regex and fell through to `parseInt` (yielding `null` or a truncated byte value), now match the tail of the string. For example, `bytes('foo1kb')` returned `null` before the change and now returns `1024` — an observable behaviour change.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "test/proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var bytes = require('..');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('fails on the patched build', function () {
    // Leading garbage must not be silently accepted; the parse regex is anchored,
    // so this falls through to parseInt and yields null.
    assert.strictEqual(bytes('foo1kb'), null);
    assert.strictEqual(bytes('garbage 42mb'), null);
  });
});
"}, "note": "Dropping the ^ anchor lets parseRegExp match a trailing number+unit anywhere in the string, so 'foo1kb' now parses to 1024 instead of falling through to parseInt and returning null. This is an observable change for malformed inputs."}
```

